{"purpose":"Record that the knowledge envelope carries no notion of staleness, so a consumer cannot distinguish current knowledge from knowledge that has quietly expired, and state the acceptance criteria.","content":"## 1. Gap\nProvenance capture is specified and implemented (`provenance`, `sourcePromptHash`, `confidence`, `captureMeta`). What is absent is any temporal validity: no field expresses when knowledge stops being trustworthy, no predicate answers whether an artifact is stale, and no mechanism flags knowledge whose surrounding facts have moved.\n\n## 2. Why this is a real problem rather than a nice-to-have\nThe standard's own strength is that knowledge outlives the session that produced it. That strength becomes a liability when an artifact outlives its own accuracy: an ADR describing a component that was since replaced is not merely outdated, it actively misleads, and it does so with the authority of an approved artifact at a high stratum. Because lower strata must not contradict higher, a stale high-stratum artifact is the most damaging possible stale artifact.\n\n## 3. Consequences today\nIntegrity checks verify structural consistency and referential resolution. They cannot report \"this describes something that no longer exists\". A consumer, human or agent, has no signal to prefer fresh knowledge over confident knowledge.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria\n- The knowledge envelope can express a validity horizon for an artifact, distinct from its creation and update dates.\n- At least one query surface answers staleness for an artifact or a domain, using the declared horizon and, where available, evidence that the described subject changed.\n- Stale artifacts are surfaced by a projection or check rather than silently included as current.\n- The semantics are additive: artifacts without a declared horizon behave exactly as they do today, so existing corpora remain conformant.\n- The rule set states whether staleness is advisory or blocking, and a test demonstrates the advisory path on an artifact whose horizon has passed."}